  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHi-Res, global and ensemble model guidance converging on a \nstorm track slightly farther east, with axis of moderate-heavy \nrain now focused across New England. A sharp gradient is \nexpected on the western side of the rainfall axis, which should \nbe placed north-south across the Hudson River Valley. Areas west\nof the Hudson Valley and north of I-90 might see little if any \nrainfall, with perhaps up to a tenth of an inch closer to I-87. \nAreas south of I-90 and near/east of I-87 and into southern VT \nshould see 0.10-0.25\", greatest across the mid Hudson Valley \ninto Litchfield County, CT, where up to 0.50\" could still occur.\n\nRain looks to develop late this afternoon (roughly 3-6 PM)\nacross the mid Hudson Valley and NW CT, and 6-9 PM slightly\nfarther N and W closer to the Capital Region and Berkshires. \nRain should taper off prior to daybreak Monday.\n\nGiven the continued eastward trends, the isolated flooding\nthreat (WPC Marginal Risk, Level 1 of 4) has now shifted east of\nour region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nCold front crosses the region Monday afternoon,\naccompanied/followed by a brief period of gusty northwest winds\nwhich could reach as high as 30-40 mph, especially within the\nMohawk Valley, Capital Region and Berkshires due to enhanced\nchanneled flow amid deeper mixing/stronger cold advection \nbehind the front. Colder air follows for Tuesday-Wednesday, with\nhigh temps mainly in the 30s to lower 40s and overnight lows in\nthe 20s except for some teens across the southern Adirondacks\nand southern VT. Other than a few passing rain/snow showers\nMonday night-Tue morning, dry conditions are expected for the\nbusy travel day on Wednesday.\n\nGuidance continues to suggest another storm system approaches\nfrom the southwest for Thursday into early Friday, however there\nremains considerable uncertainty regarding the exact storm\ntrack and precipitation types/amounts and onset timing. An \ninitially dry air mass ahead of the storm system could allow for\na brief period of wet bulb cooling as precipitation develops, \nwhich could favor snow/wintry mix at precipitation onset for \nhigher terrain areas, and perhaps even in some valley locations \nfrom Albany northward. Eventually, warmer air should allow \nprecipitation to transition to mainly rain in valleys, with rain\nor a wintry mix changing to rain for higher terrain by later \nThursday into early Friday morning. Some gusty winds may also \ndevelop as the storm system approaches, especially across some \nhigher terrain areas of western New England, where latest\n24-hour NBM probs for max wind gusts >40 mph ending 1 AM Friday \nare 40-50%.\n\n24-hour Probabilistic Winter Storm Severity Index (P-WSSI) \nending 9 AM Friday indicates 30-50% for minor impacts across \nhigher terrain areas, and 5-10% for valley areas from Albany N\nand W, suggesting some winter driving conditions are possible\nThursday or Thursday night mainly across higher elevations.",
